facebook.com/Djjayhood973","https://www.instagram.com/djjayhood"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/3865/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"3865","image":"https://i.discogs.com/bPRWOtW1vEIAJmyifaIGOwvp2Gu5_YlAkKxEIVVoVO8/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTM4NjUt/MTc4NTY5NDYxMS0y/NTQ4LmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/NKfLk79SN87F4F-OO_e0Ryqag_gQwrcpABiPLNkogvE/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTM4NjUt/MTc4NTY5NDYxMS0y/NTQ4LmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"American jazz pianist, keyboardist, composer, band leader (born April 12, 1940, in Chicago, Illinois, USA).\r\nHancock is one of the best-known modern jazz composers, creator of “Watermelon Man” (which has been a reference point throughout his career), “Maiden Voyage”, “Dolphin Dance”, right through to the dance grooves of “[r=3173760]”. Learned the piano from the age of 6, performing piano concertos by [a95546] with the [a837562] aged 11. He came to wider attention via his work with trumpeter [a20956] who introduced him to [l281] co-founder [a252962]; he recorded for Lion's label as both leader and sideman throughout the 1960s while he was a member of trumpeter [a23755]' regular working group from 1963 to 1968. Entered a period of working in jazz fusion from the late 1960s, initially with Davis, and then in his own groups in the 1970s; the \"Headhunters\" album was a best seller. Around the same time, he worked with the acoustic [a813480], effectively the Davis second quintet with [a55745] substituting.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:3865","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":270052,"name":"Ronnie Clark"},{"id":320105,"name":"Mwandishi"}],"groups":[{"active":false,"id":21399,"name":"The Headhunters"},{"active":true,"id":129530,"name":"Bahia Black"},{"active":true,"id":143887,"name":"Artists United Against Apartheid"},{"active":true,"id":262152,"name":"The Miles Davis Quintet"},{"active":true,"id":339827,"name":"Artists United For Na